game highlights and observations
================================

Good luck blessed this game:

* Entering the dungeon I knew spells of healing and identify. That fact
  alone handicapped the early game in my favor. As others have noted in
  the newsgroup, and as I concluded independently, identification is the
  core ethic of this game. In addition, as a priest(ess)'s spell power
  accumulates faster than does HP at low experience levels, zapping
  "healing" effectively inflates the available HP, by trading Power for
  HP and extending a character's life. In the early game I survived some
  pack attacks by zapping "healing" to buy more HP and thus more time.
* All of my starting attributes were 10 or higher.
* At the level above the Oracle I bought from a hardware store: oilskin
  sack, magic marker, pick-axe, blessed magic lamp, can of grease, magic
  whistle.
* I found a significant bones pile at the Oracle. I also found many
  useful items inside a silver dragon statue. Because of these
  discoveries, I had two magic lamps, two magic markers, silver dragon
  armor, and slow digestion before Medusa's Island.
* The Vibrating Square was on dungeon level 46 (Gehennom level 22). That
  made for a relatively short trip up with the Amulet.
* As a chaotic I was minimally affected by the mysterious force
  while traveling Gehennom with the Amulet. I was pulled by the undertow
  eight times; two of those landed me on the same level I just left.
* Outside the Sanctum and the Plane of Earth, the Wizard reincarnated
  only once. He never summoned pets or stole anything. And his
  malignant aura never cursed anything vital.

The only bad luck (and mild at that):

* Outside Minetown, there was only one store (level 6 hardware store).
* There was only one co-aligned altar, on dungeon level 12.
* There was no priest in the main dungeon.

Lapses of reason:

* A failed attempt to untrap a chest led to an electrical jolt.
  I was unharmed; but all of my main inventory's rings and wands
  exploded. Among the lost: my rings of slow digestion and teleport
  control. I replaced the former through a wish; I re-acquired teleport
  control first by carrying the Master Key of Thievery, then later by
  eating a tengu. This experience also induced me to use levitation
  boots (rather than a ring) in the Plane of Air.
* Spending scrolls and some magic-marker ink to create, fix, and
  enchant a crysknife. At best it had the same effectiveness as my mace.
  While the crysknife is an inherently better weapon, I had only
  "unskilled" qualification with it, versus "expert" for mace.
* Letting vampires and wraiths steal an experience level (13 down to 12)
  before I could resist. (I stepped in a level teleport trap, and
  unintentionally landed in the Valley of the Dead before I was ready.)
* Letting Asmodeus escape (he read a cursed teleportation scroll). I
  later found and killed him on Dungeon level 11.

I never had to make an emergency or life-saving prayer.

I kept my stash just inside the Sokoban entrance (off dungeon level 8).
In that level I always have two free boulders left after solving the
puzzle. I move them to block two wall openings, thereby creating two
chambers in which randomly generated monsters stay trapped. This gives
me a small protected no-teleport area for stash storage, alchemy,
polypiling, nurse dancing, and the like.

For most of the game my only weapon was the mace I began with,
eventually made rustproof and enchanted to +6. I increased my mace
qualification to "expert" on turn 5403 in Minetown. By that point my
mace attacks were taking on average about 18 HP per hit (thank you
stethoscope), far below what any adversary was taking from me. When I
got some extra enchant-weapon scrolls (just before my ascension run), I
enchanted Grayswandir to +6, tested it on some monsters, found its
average attack to be around 25 HP per hit (or more against demons and
undead), and used it exclusively during my ascension run.

I started with no pets and never acquired one. Without a magic whistle
at the start, I find pet management a nuisance. And as I could identify
any object's holy state, I lacked that major motivation for pet
ownership. I did buy a magic whistle on level 6; but even then I had
acclimated to traveling solo. Late in the game I started charming big
monsters (dragons mostly) to help occupy other monsters. But I
distinguish creatures I cultivate as pets (long-term relationships) from
those I tame as a short-term defensive tactic (love 'em and leave 'em).

Another mitigation after the first few levels: A ring of conflict is
equivalent to a herd of virtual pets during combat, in that each monster
becomes an ally while attacking other monsters. My most vivid example:
the zoo in the Wizard's Tower took about 30 turns to clear itself out as
I just slinked along the wall to the next door.

I naturally think and play a defensive, investigative, patient style.
Until my ascension run, my only weapon was my starting mace (+6). But I
was AC -4 before entering Minetown, -31 at the Castle, -37 in the Quest,
and -44 before my ascension run. Strong armor and speed boots translate
to time dilation: I effectively live in a time domain where monsters are
slow. (I'm visualizing scenes from The Matrix.) I have more time to
move, more time to act, more time to run away, more time to make
mistakes and recover.

Playing a priestess fit this style well, as a priestess cannot gain
expertise in most artifact weapons, and can't gain expertise in advanced
offensive and manipulation spells; but she can tell if an item is safe
or dangerous. I also used my stethoscope a lot to monitor monsters' HP.

And -- as so many here have highlighted -- I didn't rush. Just as
object-identification is the in-game key to success, deliberation is the
out-of-game key. The more deliberately I play, the farther I get.
Several times I'd save the game and not touch it for days. I disabled
travelling, so that I'd have to think about every step.

By the end of the game the only adversaries that could significantly
damage my HP were the Wizard and air elementals. The twin Wizards
dropped my HP from 428 to about 130 in the Sanctum before I killed them;
a full-healing potion cured that problem. After that my HP were never
seriously threatened again, except for a brief bout on the Plane of Air.
Also, I never became deathly ill, and never had to cast "cure sickness."

Dropping the Amulet before casting a spell made a serious difference in
spell-power usage. And unlike in previous games, I never forgot to pick
the Amulet back up.

The tactic of entering demon lairs from below, standing on the down
stairs, luring the demons nearby, stepping downstairs with the demons,
and standing on the up stairs below, changes those Gehennom battles into
(almost) automatic wins. (Note that I never encountered a summoned demon
prince.) I am grateful to members of this newsgroup for that tactical
pearl. I don't think that I would have figured it out on my own by now.

Earlier games taught me to resist temptation and never ever ever acquire
teleportitis without teleport control. Uncontrolled teleportation is the
younger sibling of the ascension-run mysterious force, and can just as
maddening. Conversely, I find the actual ascension run without
controlled teleportation much more tedious. The random teleport requests
(that cost no spell-power points) cut hundreds of turns from my
ascension-run time, and thereby lowered my chances of Wizard encounters.

I found two graves with bones piles four squares apart next to the
Oracle. One character died while sifting through the bones of the
second, older character; so I was pilfering through the remains of two
former lives, and recreating the scenario of the middle life. That
middle life was killed by a centaur that was still running around.

I later found the statue of a silver dragon. Breaking that open revealed
Grayswandir, Mjollnir, Excalibur, silver dragon scale mail, a blessed
luckstone, many wands and potions, and a magic lamp. From the stash I
recognized the dragon as a previous lawful valkyrie incarnation that had
stepped onto a polymorph trap, turned into a silver dragon, and stupidly
attacked a cockatrice.

Until this game I had misunderstood spell refreshing. I thought that
once you forgot a spell you could never relearn it. (I don't know why I
thought that, but I did.) So I used to closely monitor my spell
expiration times, and abort play to dash back to my spellbook stash
during the 1000-turn window between starting to forget and completely
forgetting a spell; or I'd haul spellbooks with me to avoid the dash,
even though the books were heavy and made me inevitably burdened. But
in this game I took the chance to relearn a forgotten spell I didn't
really care to use; and voila! I relearned it. This little nugget will
make future games so much more enjoyable. (I actually started playing
valkyries for a while just to get around this presumed hassle in spell
management.)

I think I'll next play a lawful priestess, mostly so that I can try out
the Sceptre of Might with expert qualification. (The artifact would also
free me from needing gray dragon mail.) On a contravening note: my
explore-mode games with lawful characters induced suicidal ideation
during the ascension run through Gehennom. (Damned mysterious force.)
I've also become quite fond of killing with abandon as a chaotic
character.

The things I don't like about playing chaotic:

* Paltry choice in artifacts, especially for characters that can't
  become skilled with swords.
* Can't drink holy water.

In my last game before this one (explore mode), I died only once, in the
Plane of Water. I had my conflict ring on, and was resting inside some
benign vortex. But thanks to the conflict the vortex was killed, and I
was abandoned outside an air bubble. I immediately drowned. Given that
scare, I switched to the magical breathing amulet while in Water. (I
don't think reflection is a factor in Water.)

Before reading about the same tactic in the newsgroup, I decided to cut
wide direct inter-stair paths in Gehennom. But in my recent games I've
also cut such paths in the main dungeon. This helps minimize exposure to
the Wizard during the ascension run. It also serves as a clue if I 
suffer amnesia.

I carried too much stuff to the Planes. I used maybe 5% of my wands. I
brought too much food: I only needed to eat once during the entire
ascension run. Being "skilled" at clerical spells meant I could remove
the curse from my entire inventory, and thus didn't need all of the holy
water I brought: I never used a single potion during the ascension run.

I bought priest protection in stages as I acquired money. I have never
been tempted to zip down to Minetown at XL 1. I find other ways to
acquire early AC,  mostly through found or bought armor. Also, by the
time I can defeat nymphs, I can use their object-detection potions to
find vaults and pay for protection at higher prices. Until I read about
it in the newsgroup, it never occurred to me that players would
purposely stay weak just to buy protection cheaply. Such a tactic also
requires a strong pet from the beginning, in contrast with my personal
style and preference.

I gained five experience levels from eating wraiths on the quest. I love
the priestess quest. The nemesis is easy to kill, the graveyard
inhabitants are slow and easy to kill, the dead wraiths give experience
gain, and the graveyards are filled with boxes of goodies. The boxes in
graveyards are like Christmas presents at Halloween, sort of the reverse
from "A Nightmare Before Christmas."

I now believe that once a priest or priestess knows "identify,"
"healing," and "remove curse," and acquires enough spell-casting power
to use them regularly, the bias toward winning is so high that only
truly bad luck or stupid play can kill the character.

In close, I want to again thank you spoiler authors and source divers
and other sages for your help. I ascended only four months after first
encountering this game, and less than two months after playing outside
explore mode. Were it not for the spoilers and this group, I'd be stuck
on level 5 forever.

